{"id":"f20951d0-3210-4b51-9b31-5c7e9326bf64","object":"chat.completion","created":1785031148,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if \\\"threshold\\\" not in case or \\\"calls\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        required = [\\\"call_id\\\", \\\"loss_without_call\\\", \\\"loss_with_call\\\", \\\"prediction_tokens\\\", \\\"parseable\\\"]\\n        if not all(k in call for k in required):\\n            raise ValueError\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        gain = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"] - call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        norm_tokens = max(call[\\\"prediction_tokens\\\"], 1)\\n        normalized_gain = round(gain / norm_tokens, 8)\\n        accepted = bool(call[\\\"parseable\\\"]) and normalized_gain >= threshold\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\\"accepted\\\": accepted, \\\"call_id\\\": call_id, \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain})\\n    return {\\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids, \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write the `solve(case)` function based on the task description.\n\nThe task is \"AGENT-TF-01\" and from the description, it seems to be about filtering API calls based on loss values. The provided fixture cases show payloads with a threshold and a list of calls, each with call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable.\n\nWe need to compute for each call:\n- gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call\n- normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens (or at least one? The instruction says \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" So if prediction_tokens is 0? But in the data, it's positive integers, so we can divide by prediction_tokens). Actually \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" could mean that if prediction_tokens is less than 1, we use 1, but since it's always >=2 in fixtures, we can just divide by prediction_tokens. But to be safe, we can use max(prediction_tokens, 1).\n- Then check if parseable is True and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n- If accepted, include call_id in accepted_call_ids, and for each call (in order) include a diagnostic object with: accepted (bool), call_id, normalized_gain (number).\n\nThe output must match the JSON schema exactly: accepted_call_ids list of strings, diagnostics list of objects with accepted, call_id, normalized_gain.\n\nAlso, the function must be named solve(case) and return a dict.\n\nWe need to ensure we don't mutate case. Also, we must handle any missing fields? The description says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check that threshold exists, calls list exists, each call has required fields. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nEdge cases: threshold can be any number. Normalized_gain might be negative? The gain is loss_without - loss_with, which can be negative if loss_with > loss_without. But then normalized_gain would be negative, and threshold positive (0.05) maybe insufficient. But we follow the rule: accepted if normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\nAlso, note that the diagnostics order must be the same as input calls order. We'll iterate through calls in order, compute normalized_gain, then check if parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\nNow, implement solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case\n    if \"threshold\" not in case or \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required keys\")\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be list\")\n    \n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    \n    for call in calls:\n        # Validate each call has required fields\n        required_call_fields = [\"call_id\", \"loss_without_call\", \"loss_with_call\", \"prediction_tokens\", \"parseable\"]\n        if not all(f in call for f in required_call_fields):\n            raise ValueError(\"Call missing required fields\")\n        \n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        prediction_tokens = call[\"prediction_tokens\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        # Normalize by at least one prediction token\n        norm_tokens = max(prediction_tokens, 1)\n        normalized_gain = gain / norm_tokens\n        \n        accepted = bool(parseable) and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        \n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"accepted\": accepted,\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain\n        })\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n\nWe must output as a string of Python code. The submission envelope expects a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string. So we need to write that.\n\nWe must also respect the exact output structural schema. Note that normalized_gain should be a number, not necessarily rounded? The description says \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" but the schema just says number. Looking at fixtures, the values are given with many decimals. Probably we should round? The instruction says \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" So we should round normalized_gain to some precision? It doesn't specify. The schema says \"normalized_gain\": {\"type\": \"number\"} so we can leave as float. But to be safe, we can round to 10 decimal places? But the examples in diagnostics are not provided; we don't have expected outputs. The instruction says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" but no precision given. I think we should round to a reasonable precision like 6 or 8 decimal places? However, the output must match expected values from test cases. Since we don't have expected outputs, we must decide. The phrase \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" might mean we should round the normalized_gain to some number of decimal places? But it's ambiguous. Possibly they want to round to the same precision as input? The input losses have 8 decimal places. We can round to 8 decimal places. But to be safe, we can round to 10 decimal places. Alternatively, we can not round and let it be the exact float. However, Python floating point may have representation issues. I think we should round to a fixed number like 8 decimal places to avoid floating point artifacts. But the schema doesn't require rounding. Given the instruction \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" I'll assume we need to round to a reasonable number of decimal places, e.g., 10. But we don't know the expected output. Alternatively, we can round to 6 decimal places. I'll check the fixture values: loss values have 8 decimal places. So rounding to 8 decimals seems natural.\n\nBut the task also says \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" which we are doing.\n\nI'll implement rounding to 8 decimal places using round(normalized_gain, 8).\n\nAlso, note that we need to ensure the function returns a JSON-serializable object, which it does.\n\nNow, we need to output the Python source as a string. We must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes for JSON.\n\nPut the implementation inside a single line? Better to write multi-line string and then escape it.\n\nSo the final JSON response:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # ... code ...\\n\"}\n\nWe need to ensure the string is valid JSON.\n\nWrite the Python code with proper indentation, using double spaces or tabs? We can use 4 spaces.\n\nBut careful: Inside JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and quotes.\n\nLet's write the implementation as a raw string with explicit escapes.\n\nAlternatively, we can write the code as a single line with semicolons but that's messy.\n\nBetter to write a properly formatted multi-line string in Python and then use json.dumps to generate the JSON. But we are not executing Python; we are just writing the response text. So we need to manually escape.\n\nGiven the response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\", we can write it like:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case.get('threshold')\\n    ...\"}\n\nBut we must escape newlines as \\n and double quotes as \\\". We'll do that.\n\nI'll write the code as a string with escaped characters.\n\nLet me draft the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    if \"threshold\" not in case or \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        required = [\"call_id\", \"loss_without_call\", \"loss_with_call\", \"prediction_tokens\", \"parseable\"]\n        if not all(k in call for k in required):\n            raise ValueError\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        gain = call[\"loss_without_call\"] - call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        norm_tokens = max(call[\"prediction_tokens\"], 1)\n        normalized_gain = round(gain / norm_tokens, 8)\n        accepted = bool(call[\"parseable\"]) and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n        diagnostics.append({\"accepted\": accepted, \"call_id\": call_id, \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain})\n    return {\"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids, \"diagnostics\": diagnostics}\n\nNote: Use round(..., 8) as discussed. But could also not round. I'll go with round to 8 decimal places to satisfy \"rounded per-call diagnostics\".\n\nNow, we need to put this into a JSON string.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if \\\"threshold\\\" not in case or \\\"calls\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        required = [\\\"call_id\\\", \\\"loss_without_call\\\", \\\"loss_with_call\\\", \\\"prediction_tokens\\\", \\\"parseable\\\"]\\n        if not all(k in call for k in required):\\n            raise ValueError\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        gain = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"] - call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        norm_tokens = max(call[\\\"prediction_tokens\\\"], 1)\\n        normalized_gain = round(gain / norm_tokens, 8)\\n        accepted = bool(call[\\\"parseable\\\"]) and normalized_gain >= threshold\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\\"accepted\\\": accepted, \\\"call_id\\\": call_id, \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain})\\n    return {\\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids, \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics}\\n\"}\n\nBut we need to escape double quotes inside strings: they become \\\". Also newlines as \\n. That's done.\n\nNow, ensure that the entire response is exactly that JSON. No trailing spaces, no markdown.\n\nI'll output it."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2164,"completion_tokens":2523,"total_tokens":4687,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2236},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":116},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}